  "review": {
    "review": "The initiating attrition followed waking a prison guard with the last staff charge: decision 2549 (accepted line 7465) pulled the hero into contact and inflicted cripple. Melee, a small dew drink and a two-turn retreat left 14 HP. During the subsequent escape, decision 2554 (line 7479) continued toward a formerly separated cover destination even though the following guard could now see it. The committed-cover branch omitted endpoint checks already required for fresh guard-cover selection. A partial offline helper reconstruction matches decisions 2553-2554; it is not recovered original runtime memory. The proposed correction reapplies those existing endpoint checks before continuing. Three new regression tests pass after two expected baseline assertion failures; the full suite passes 2,044 tests with four skips. This does not establish lasting escape or death prevention; the first movement remains unchanged. The final accepted transition at line 7639 records death, and the full terminal public message explicitly identifies a crazy thief as the killer. Unknown potion identities were not treated as pre-death resources. The adapter will derive the changed policy and new test from the working tree; files is intentionally empty.",
    "learnings": "A following guard can invalidate a cover destination before arrival. Recheck the destination as well as the route on every committed escape step. Currently admitted cover is not proof of lasting safety or successful escape."
  },
